          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly avows her love for Lear as a present emotional and relational state. The surrounding qualification limits its extent but does not remove the truth-conditional avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal is qualified as love owed under an existing filial bond, so it invokes an established relational obligation. It does not clearly make a new promise or undertake a distinct future action; Cordelia presents herself as fulfilling duties already binding on her.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled, measured avowal rather than a reflex-like discharge of emotion. Cordelia explicitly describes difficulty verbalizing her heart and then carefully bounds what she says.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.89,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a restrained refusal to compete in Lear's demanded performance of superlative love. The limiting formula corrects the premise that Cordelia should claim unlimited or exclusive love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public court ceremony, Lear asks his daughters to say how much they love him while linking their answers to the division of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this measured avowal to Lear, qualifying it by her filial bond rather than adopting her sisters’ expansive rhetoric.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing daughter-to-father bond and the duties of obedience, love, and honour that she regards as arising from it. She does not clearly create a new commitment in the focal words. Lear does not accept the bounded formulation as an adequate response: he tells her to “Mend your speech a little,” so there is no affirmative uptake of her framing in this exchange.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as William Shakespeare’s King Lear, including the opening love test and the later reconciliation and final scene.\",\"limitations\":\"The focal clause itself is a short avowal; its obligation-invoking and resistant functions depend on its immediate qualification and the represented love-test setting. The supplied later passages support genuine filial affection, but they do not convert the focal avowal into a new promise or prove every aspect of Cordelia’s inward state at the exact moment of speaking.\"}"
